{
 "lead": "A Fourier transform turns a signal x(t) into a spectrum X(ω). Many operations that are hard in time become easy algebra in frequency. Shifting a signal adds a phase ramp. Convolving with an impulse response becomes multiplying spectra. Multiplying by a cosine copies and halves the spectrum. Engineers use these rules for filters, radio transmitters, sound tones, and power-supply noise checks. This chapter lists the main properties, proves the convolution and modulation theorems, and shows how to compute transforms without long integrals.",
 "prereq": "You need: the <a href=\"#fourier-transform-of-aperiodic-signals\">Fourier transform and its standard pairs</a> and the convolution integral (see <i>Convolution integral and its evaluation</i>).",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Core transform properties",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>X(ω) = ∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup> x(t)e<sup>−jωt</sup>dt</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here x(t) is the signal; X(ω) is its spectrum; ω is angular frequency in rad/s; j is the imaginary unit."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The table gives the rules used most often. Here a and b are constants (in the scaling row a is any nonzero real number), t₀ is a time shift in seconds, ω₀ is a frequency shift in rad/s, and δ is the unit impulse. Each row is proved by putting the left side into the defining integral and substituting."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "Property",
      "Time domain",
      "Frequency domain"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Linearity",
       "a x₁(t)+b x₂(t)",
       "aX₁(ω)+bX₂(ω)"
      ],
      [
       "Time shift",
       "x(t−t₀)",
       "e<sup>−jωt₀</sup>X(ω)"
      ],
      [
       "Frequency shift",
       "e<sup>jω₀t</sup>x(t)",
       "X(ω−ω₀)"
      ],
      [
       "Scaling",
       "x(at)",
       "(1/|a|)X(ω/a)"
      ],
      [
       "Time reversal",
       "x(−t)",
       "X(−ω)"
      ],
      [
       "Derivative",
       "dx/dt",
       "jωX(ω)"
      ],
      [
       "Frequency derivative",
       "t x(t)",
       "j dX(ω)/dω"
      ],
      [
       "Integration",
       "∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>t</sup>x(τ)dτ",
       "X(ω)/(jω)+πX(0)δ(ω)"
      ],
      [
       "Duality",
       "X(t)",
       "2πx(−ω)"
      ],
      [
       "Conjugation",
       "x*(t)",
       "X*(−ω)"
      ],
      [
       "Convolution",
       "x(t)*h(t)",
       "X(ω)H(ω)"
      ],
      [
       "Multiplication",
       "x(t)y(t)",
       "(1/2π)X(ω)*Y(ω)"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For real x(t), the spectrum obeys X(−ω)=X*(ω). The magnitude is even and the phase is odd. An even real signal has a real even spectrum. An odd real signal has an imaginary odd spectrum. These symmetry facts let you check an answer quickly."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Convolution theorem: time convolution becomes spectral product",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Convolution in time is defined as y(t)=x(t)*h(t)=∫<sub>−∞</sub><sup>∞</sup>x(τ)h(t−τ)dτ. The convolution theorem says the transform of y(t) is the product of the two transforms."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>x(t)*h(t) ↔ X(ω)H(ω)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here x(t) is the input; h(t) is the impulse response; X(ω) and H(ω) are their spectra; * means convolution."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Write Y(ω)=∫∫ x(τ)h(t−τ)e<sup>−jωt</sup>dτdt.",
      "Substitute u=t−τ, so t=u+τ and dt=du.",
      "Then e<sup>−jω(u+τ)</sup>=e<sup>−jωu</sup>e<sup>−jωτ</sup>.",
      "The double integral separates into ∫x(τ)e<sup>−jωτ</sup>dτ times ∫h(u)e<sup>−jωu</sup>du.",
      "The result is Y(ω)=X(ω)H(ω)."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The dual rule is multiplication in time: x(t)y(t) ↔ (1/2π)X(ω)*Y(ω). The factor 1/2π comes from the inverse-transform convention. This rule explains why a short window smears a spectrum."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Modulation theorem: cosine makes two shifted copies",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Use Euler's identity cos(ω₀t)=(e<sup>jω₀t</sup>+e<sup>−jω₀t</sup>)/2. Multiply x(t) by this cosine. The frequency-shift rule gives one copy moved to ω₀ and one copy moved to −ω₀."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>x(t)cos(ω₀t) ↔ ½[X(ω−ω₀)+X(ω+ω₀)]</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here ω₀ is the carrier frequency in rad/s; X(ω) is the original spectrum; each shifted copy has half the original height."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The shift rule itself is e<sup>jω₀t</sup>x(t) ↔ X(ω−ω₀). The modulation theorem uses a cosine, so it applies two shifts. Some syllabi call modulation the frequency-shifting property. The cosine version is the one used for amplitude modulation."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A radio station multiplies voice by a carrier cosine. The voice spectrum moves to the carrier frequency. A receiver multiplies by a local cosine and moves it back. The half-size copies carry the same information."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
