 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "Find the Fourier transform of x(t)=e<sup>−2t</sup>cos(10t)u(t), where t is in seconds and x(t) is in volts. Also give X(0) in V·s.",
   "steps": [
    "Take the base signal g(t)=e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t). Its transform is G(ω)=1/(2+jω), with ω in rad/s.",
    "Write x(t)=g(t)cos(10t). The carrier frequency is ω₀=10 rad/s.",
    "Apply the modulation theorem: X(ω)=½[G(ω−10)+G(ω+10)].",
    "Substitute G: X(ω)=½[1/(2+j(ω−10))+1/(2+j(ω+10))].",
    "At ω=0, X(0)=½[1/(2−j10)+1/(2+j10)].",
    "Combine the fractions: (2+j10+2−j10)/(2²+10²)=4/104.",
    "So X(0)=½×4/104=2/104=1/52≈0.0192 V·s. Direct check: ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup>e<sup>−2t</sup>cos(10t)dt = 2/(2²+10²) = 2/104, the same value."
   ],
   "answer": "X(ω)=½[1/(2+j(ω−10))+1/(2+j(ω+10))] V·s, and at ω=0, X(0)=1/52≈0.0192 V·s.",
   "check": "import cmath\nw0 = 10.0\nw = 0.0\nX = 0.5*(1/(2 + 1j*(w - w0)) + 1/(2 + 1j*(w + w0)))\nprint(X.real)",
   "values": [
    0.019230769230769232
   ],
   "output": ""
  },
  {
   "question": "Use the convolution theorem to find y(t) = x(t)*h(t) for x(t) = e<sup>−t</sup>u(t) and h(t) = e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t). Give y(1 s) and the peak of y(t).",
   "steps": [
    "Transforms: X(ω) = 1/(1+jω) and H(ω) = 1/(2+jω).",
    "Convolution theorem: Y(ω) = X(ω)H(ω) = 1/[(1+jω)(2+jω)].",
    "Partial fractions in s = jω: 1/[(1+s)(2+s)] = A/(1+s) + B/(2+s). At s = −1: A = 1/(2−1) = 1. At s = −2: B = 1/(1−2) = −1.",
    "So Y(ω) = 1/(1+jω) − 1/(2+jω), and from the standard pair, y(t) = (e<sup>−t</sup> − e<sup>−2t</sup>)u(t).",
    "At t = 1 s: y(1) = e<sup>−1</sup> − e<sup>−2</sup> = 0.3679 − 0.1353 = 0.2325.",
    "Peak: dy/dt = −e<sup>−t</sup> + 2e<sup>−2t</sup> = 0 gives e<sup>−t</sup> = ½, t = ln 2 = 0.693 s, and y = ½ − ¼ = 0.25.",
    "Check in time: ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>t</sup>e<sup>−τ</sup>e<sup>−2(t−τ)</sup>dτ = e<sup>−2t</sup>(e<sup>t</sup> − 1) = e<sup>−t</sup> − e<sup>−2t</sup>. Also Y(0) = ½ equals the area ∫y dt = 1 − ½ = ½."
   ],
   "answer": "y(t) = (e<sup>−t</sup> − e<sup>−2t</sup>)u(t); y(1) = 0.2325, and the peak is 0.25 at t = ln 2 ≈ 0.693 s."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Wrong sign in frequency shift: e<sup>jω₀t</sup>x(t) shifts to X(ω−ω₀), not X(ω+ω₀).",
  "Forgetting the 1/2π factor in the time-multiplication rule x(t)y(t) ↔ (1/2π)X(ω)*Y(ω).",
  "Missing the 1/|a| factor in scaling: x(at) ↔ (1/|a|)X(ω/a).",
  "Using X(ω)+H(ω) or X(ω)*H(ω) in frequency for time convolution; the correct result is the product X(ω)H(ω).",
  "Wrong phase sign in time shift: x(t−t₀) gives e<sup>−jωt₀</sup>X(ω), not e<sup>+jωt₀</sup>X(ω)."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the modulation theorem for x(t)cos(ω₀t).",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "x(t)cos(ω₀t) ↔ ½[X(ω−ω₀)+X(ω+ω₀)]."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does the convolution theorem make LTI system analysis easier?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The output spectrum is Y(ω)=X(ω)H(ω). Multiply the input spectrum by the frequency response, then inverse-transform. No time convolution integral is needed."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "If x(t) ↔ X(ω)=1/(1+jω), find the transform of x(t−2).",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "Use the time-shift rule. x(t−2) ↔ e<sup>−j2ω</sup>X(ω)=e<sup>−j2ω</sup>/(1+jω)."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Find X(ω) for x(t)=e<sup>−2t</sup>u(t)cos(20t).",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "The base transform is 1/(2+jω). With ω₀=20, X(ω)=½[1/(2+j(ω−20))+1/(2+j(ω+20))]."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "A real signal has spectrum X(ω). Explain why |X(ω)| is even and ∠X(ω) is odd.",
   "marks": 5,
   "a": "For real x(t), X(−ω)=X*(ω). Taking magnitudes gives |X(−ω)|=|X*(ω)|=|X(ω)|. The phase changes sign: ∠X(−ω)=−∠X(ω)."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "X(ω) is zero for |ω|>B. The signal is multiplied by cos(ω₀t). What spectral bands appear, and what condition avoids overlap?",
   "marks": 6,
   "a": "The copies occupy [ω₀−B,ω₀+B] and [−ω₀−B,−ω₀+B]. They do not overlap when ω₀>B. Each copy has half the original height."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. In software radio, the modulation theorem moves baseband samples to a carrier and back using digital mixers. OFDM transmitters use inverse FFT, and fast filtering uses FFT convolution. Search 'digital upconversion', 'OFDM modulation', 'FFT convolution', and 'analytic signal'. Modern curricula connect these properties to the short-time Fourier transform and wavelets."
